notes.body:
The process where a bunch of threads are blocked in `read()` is the parent; I think we fire up several threads to run various extcap processes in parallel to fill in the capture device list.

The other process is a child, presumably forked off to run an extcap process.  It appears to be at a line that's calling `signal()`; I *guess* this is the first time `signal()` (as opposed to `sigaction()`) is being called, so the run-time linker is trying to find `signal()` in the system dynamically-linked library (or, as this is Big Sur, in the big library cache file).

The child is blocked trying to grab some global mutex that the run-time linker has, in order to make sure no other thread unloads some library at the same time, at least as I read the code in question:

```
	// <rdar://problem/8663923> race condition with flat-namespace lazy binding
	if ( this->usesTwoLevelNameSpace() ) {
		// two-level namespace lookup does not require lock because dependents can't be unloaded before this image
	}
	else {
		// acquire dyld global lock
		if ( lock != NULL )
			lock();
	}
```

Why the global lock appears to be held is a good question.

I guess the questions are:

1. whether you trust the libffi developers or Apple to keep libffi working on all Macs;
1. whether you want to add another third-party library;
1. whether Apple's libffi lacks any features that Glib 2.68.2 requires in order to implement some optional Glib features such that Wireshark uses.

Currently, the Wireshark app bundle doesn't include libffi and doesn't appear to provide any dylibs that use it.
